course: dictionary
lesson: 1927
title: Comfrey, a plant from the riverbank
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Comfrey is a tall wild plant with large hairy leaves and bell-shaped flowers, which can be purple, pink or white. It grows in damp places, such as by rivers and in ditches.
explain: You may not know comfrey, but the words around it give clues. In The Wind in the Willows the Mole and the Rat pass «purple loosestrife», «willow-herb» and comfrey along the river. Those plants all grow in wet ground, so comfrey must grow there too.
explain: Context can also tell you what kind of thing a word is. Gardeners make «comfrey tea» as plant food, so comfrey is a plant, not a drink for people. Ask yourself: is this a thing, a place, a person or an action? The clues will often tell you.
fact: Comfrey means a tall wild plant with large hairy leaves and bell-shaped flowers.
fact: Comfrey grows in damp places by rivers and ditches.
fact: Long ago, people used comfrey leaves and roots as a herbal medicine for bruises and broken bones.
fact: Gardeners still use comfrey to feed the soil.
fact: Comfrey should never be eaten, because it can harm the liver.
exercise: «The river bank was full of comfrey, purple loosestrife and willow-herb.» What kind of thing is comfrey? | A river | *A plant | A jacket | A bird that sings
exercise: Comfrey grows in which kind of place? | Hot, dusty roads | *Damp places by rivers and ditches | Bowls in the kitchen | Dry sand in the desert
exercise: Gardeners make «comfrey tea». What is it for? | *To feed the soil as plant food | To wash the car | To make a loud noise | To drink with biscuits
test: Why should you never eat comfrey? | It is too big to fit in a bowl | *It can harm your liver | It only grows in winter | It tastes too sweet
test: Comfrey had an old name, «knitbone». Why? | It was grown in knitting shops | *People believed it helped bones heal | It makes wool soft | Its leaves look like knitted wool
exam: Which words show where comfrey grows? | «the play was ready to begin» | «diffident and delaying» | *«along the river» | «a jerkin, an old short jacket»
